# Stacked notes for `apps/patterns`

## Context

Pattern descriptions in `apps/patterns/` currently render one-at-a-time on `/patterns/[...slug]`. Patterns reference each other heavily through plain MDX links, so reading a description usually means following a chain and losing the original context to a full page swap. We want a *stacked notes* presentation (Andy Matuschak's working notes, Maggie Appleton's garden) where clicking an internal pattern link slides a new pane in to the right of the source pane, the source remains visible, and the URL records the full stack so it stays shareable.

Goal: keep the existing static-Astro pipeline intact, layer stacking on top as a thin client behaviour, and degrade gracefully on small screens.

## Decisions (already confirmed)

- URL encoding: repeated `?stackedNotes=<slug>` query params (Andy-style). Example: `/patterns/operations/undo?stackedNotes=activities/foo&stackedNotes=qualities/bar`.
- Sidebar clicks reset the stack to a single pane (the chosen pattern). Sidebar is a *jump*, not a *push*.
- Mobile (<768px) collapses to a single pane; `history.back()` walks the stack back.
- Truncate-and-append on link clicks: clicking a link inside pane *N* drops everything to the right of *N* and appends the target as pane *N+1* (Andy/Matuschak convention).

## Architecture

We keep the current static build. Each `/patterns/<slug>` page still server-renders the full page with one pane (the leftmost). A small React island, `StackManager`, mounts inside the `AppShell` slot and is responsible for:

1. Parsing `?stackedNotes=…` on mount and asynchronously fetching the static HTML of each additional pane.
2. Intercepting clicks on internal pattern links (`<a href="/patterns/…">`) inside any pane, fetching the target, and pushing it as a new pane (truncate-and-append).
3. Maintaining stack state in a Zustand store and syncing it to `history.pushState` / `popstate`.
4. Managing focus, scroll-into-view, and pane visual depth (dim non-active panes).
5. Collapsing to single-pane on mobile via a CSS media query.

### Three integration constraints to handle up front

These come out of a design review and would derail a naive implementation:

1. *`<ClientRouter />` is enabled in `Base.astro`.* That means Astro owns `<a href="/patterns/…">` clicks via a bubble-phase listener and a `navigate` event. A plain click delegate won't beat it. We integrate by registering a listener for `astro:before-preparation` — Astro's pre-navigation hook — and calling `e.preventDefault()` when the destination is an internal pattern URL and the click originated from inside a `[data-pane-index]` element. This is the idiomatic escape hatch and runs before ClientRouter swaps anything.

2. *ClientRouter performs soft navigations.* So when the user clicks a sidebar link, the page swaps without a real reload — a module-level Zustand store would *persist* and the stack wouldn't reset. Fix: listen for `astro:page-load` (fires after every soft navigation and on first load) and run `store.syncFromURL()` there. If the new URL has no `stackedNotes` params, the store resets to a single pane equal to the path slug.

3. *`AppShell` is `client:only="react"`.* Astro renders the slot content during SSR for first paint, but the React island wipes that DOM when it hydrates. We therefore can't rely on the SSR'd `<article>` surviving as "pane 0". Instead we embed the initial pane as an inert JSON blob that lives *outside* the island, and `StackManager` reads it on mount:

   ```astro
   <!-- Base.astro -->
   <AppShell client:only="react" ...><slot /></AppShell>
   {initialPane && (
     <script type="application/json" id="initial-pane" set:html={JSON.stringify(initialPane)} />
   )}
   ```

   `[...slug].astro` produces `initialPane = { slug, title, html }` by rendering `<Content />` to a string with Astro's experimental Container API (`experimental_AstroContainer.create()` + `renderToString(Content)`). The page also keeps `<Content />` inside the AppShell slot, so first paint still shows the real article — it just gets replaced by the React-rendered pane 0 on hydration. Tradeoff: a brief swap on hydration. Acceptable. If the Container API turns out to be impractical, the fallback is to render the article into a `<template id="initial-pane-html">` element outside AppShell and read its `.content` on mount — same effect, no API risk.

Sidebar links stay plain anchors. ClientRouter handles the navigation; `astro:page-load` reconciles the store.

### Pane fetching strategy

We do not need a separate partial endpoint. Each pattern URL already statically renders a self-contained article. The client fetches `'/patterns/<slug>/'` with `fetch()`, parses with `DOMParser`, and extracts the first `<article>` element. This avoids duplicating any rendering pipeline and reuses the MDX output Astro already builds. Responses are cached in the Zustand store keyed by slug so revisits within a session are instant.

### State shape (Zustand)

```ts
type Pane = {
  slug: string;            // e.g. "operations/undo"
  title: string;           // pulled from the fetched <h1>
  html: string;            // the <article>…</article> inner HTML
  status: 'loading' | 'ready' | 'error';
};

type StackState = {
  panes: Pane[];                          // ordered left-to-right
  activeSlug: string | null;              // rightmost-visible on desktop, current on mobile
  cache: Map<string, Pane>;               // O(1) lookup; Map per state-management rule
  push(slug: string, fromIndex: number): Promise<void>;
  pop(toIndex: number): void;
  reset(slug: string): Promise<void>;
  syncFromURL(): Promise<void>;
};
```

Uses `Map` for the cache (per `.claude/rules/state-management.md`). No `persist` middleware — stack state is URL-derived, not user-preference.

### URL <-> state sync

- On `astro:page-load` (also fires on first load): run `syncFromURL`. Path slug → pane 0 (hydrated from the `#initial-pane` JSON blob when the path matches it, otherwise fetched); `searchParams.getAll('stackedNotes')` → panes 1..N, fetched in parallel.
- On `push`: `history.pushState({}, '', buildURL(panes))`.
- On `popstate`: re-run `syncFromURL`.
- Sidebar nav: ClientRouter swaps the page → `astro:page-load` fires → store reconciles to the new URL (which has no `stackedNotes`) → stack is a single pane. No special-casing needed.

`buildURL` keeps the path component as the *first* pane's slug and emits one `stackedNotes` param per subsequent pane.

### Click interception (via ClientRouter hook)

Register on `document` for `astro:before-preparation`. The handler:

- Reads `event.from`, `event.to`, and `event.sourceElement` (an `<a>`).
- Bails if `event.sourceElement` has no enclosing `[data-pane-index]` (i.e., the click came from the sidebar or elsewhere) — let ClientRouter do its full navigation.
- Bails if modifier keys/middle-click are encoded in the event, or if the link carries `data-no-stack`.
- Bails if the target URL is not under `/patterns/`.
- Otherwise calls `event.preventDefault()` (cancels the soft nav) and `stack.push(targetSlug, fromIndex)`.

Result: in-pane links stack, sidebar/external links do their normal navigation.

### Layout & styling

- New stylesheet `apps/patterns/src/styles/stack.css` (loaded from `Base.astro`).
- `<main>` becomes the stack container: `display: flex; overflow-x: auto; scroll-snap-type: x proximity`.
- Each `.pane`: `flex: 0 0 var(--pane-width)` (default `min(48rem, 100%)`), `scroll-snap-align: start`, padding consistent with current 60ch article width.
- Non-active panes get `opacity: .55; filter: saturate(.8)` and a slight scale-down to convey depth; transition on opacity (avoid `filter` on the scroll path — use `will-change: opacity`).
- Optional sticky "spine": rotated title on the right edge of each pane, `position: sticky; right: 0`, surfaces when panes are scrolled past. Nice-to-have; ship without if it complicates the layout.
- Below `@media (max-width: 768px)`: container becomes single-column, only the active pane is `display: block`; others are `display: none`. Back button (browser native) pops the stack via `popstate`.

The existing `app.css` rule `main { margin: 2rem 3rem; max-width: 60ch }` must be scoped to non-stack pages or removed — patterns now want the full inset width. Easiest: move the margin/max-width onto `.pane > article` instead of `main`.

### Accessibility

- Each pane is `<section role="region" aria-label="Pattern: {title}" aria-current={isActive ? 'true' : undefined}>`.
- On pane push: `setTimeout(() => paneEl.querySelector('h1').focus(), 0)` after scroll. Make `h1` programmatically focusable with `tabindex="-1"`.
- `prefers-reduced-motion`: disable scroll smoothing and opacity transitions.
- Skip-link in the AppShell stays pointing at `<main>`; once focus enters, normal tab order flows through panes left-to-right.
- Scroll restoration on deep-link load: after all panes are fetched, `scrollIntoView({ inline: 'end', behavior: 'instant' })` on the rightmost pane.

### Caveats on fetched-pane content

Fetched pane HTML is injected via React's `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (we trust our own static output). Implications:

- *Lit web components* (registered globally in `register-all.ts`) auto-upgrade on insertion via the customElements registry — fine, they'll work in any pane.
- *React/Astro islands inside MDX*, if any pattern uses them, will NOT rehydrate when injected as HTML. They render as static markup. Before implementation, grep `apps/patterns/src/content/patterns/**/*.mdx` for `client:` directives or React component imports; if there are any, the affected patterns either need to stay as the leftmost (SSR'd) pane only, or we accept the static-only degradation as a documented behaviour.
- *Article-internal styles* (`.description`, `.badge`, `.meta` on the pattern page) must live in global CSS, not scoped to `[...slug].astro`. Astro-scoped styles get a per-component hash class that the fetched HTML won't carry. Pre-implementation check: confirm those rules are in `apps/patterns/src/styles/app.css` or `packages/components/src/styles/*`, not in a `<style>` block in `[...slug].astro`. If they are scoped, move them to `stack.css` or `app.css`.

